#
# Two separate concerns live in this file. The merge section, which is ACTIVE,
# changes how Unity's own text files are combined when two members edit the
# same scene. The LFS section, which is NOT, would change how binaries are
# stored - and the comment there records why it is not switched on yet.
#
# ======================================================================
# Unity SmartMerge - scene, prefab and asset merges        [ACTIVE]
# ======================================================================
# A .unity file is YAML, so Git merges it line by line and can produce a file
# that parses cleanly but describes a scene nobody built - two members move
# different objects, and the result quietly loses one of them.
# UnityYAMLMerge understands the format and merges by object instead.
#
# The driver is a path to an executable, so it cannot live in this file -
# each member registers it once on their own machine, pointing at their own
# Unity install. On this project's editor version that is:
#
#     git config merge.unityyamlmerge.name "Unity SmartMerge"
#     git config merge.unityyamlmerge.driver "'D:/Unity/6000.6.2f1/Editor/Data/Tools/UnityYAMLMerge.exe' merge -p --force --fallback ondemand %O %B %A %A"
#     git config merge.unityyamlmerge.recursive binary
#
# Until it is registered Git falls back to its normal merge, so a machine
# that has not run those three lines is no worse off than before this file
# existed - it simply does not get the better merge.
*.unity  merge=unityyamlmerge
*.asset  merge=unityyamlmerge
*.prefab merge=unityyamlmerge

# Unity's formats are text and must stay diffable. Marking them explicitly
# also keeps line endings consistent across the three machines on this
# project, which is what stops a scene showing as wholly rewritten when it
# was opened on a different one.
*.unity text
*.asset text
*.meta  text

# ======================================================================
# Git LFS - large binary assets                      [NOT SWITCHED ON]
# ======================================================================
# The case for it is real. The tracked project is about 443 MB and binaries
# are almost all of it - 425 .wav at 272.8 MB, 356 .png at 157.2 MB - and
# plain Git stores a complete new copy of a binary on every change, so
# re-exporting one 30 MB music track five times adds 150 MB permanently.
#
# The three rules that would do it are written below, commented out. They
# are commented out because switching them on NOW, in this repository, makes
# things worse rather than better, and that was measured rather than guessed:
#
#   Turning them on marks all 807 binaries already in the project as
#   modified, because Git compares the working file against the stored blob
#   THROUGH the new filter. Committing that converts the whole 443 MB into
#   LFS objects in a single commit - while every original blob stays in the
#   history, where it already is. The repository gets BIGGER, and 443 MB of
#   new LFS objects does not fit in GitHub's 1 GB free LFS allowance.
#
#   Shrinking the history instead needs `git lfs migrate import --everything`,
#   which rewrites every commit and changes every hash. On a repository three
#   people are working in, with open branches, that is a coordinated
#   operation for a quiet week, not a convenience.
#
# So the honest state is: appropriate for this project, worth doing, and
# correctly done either at the start of a project or during a planned
# migration - neither of which is now. Every member must also have run
# `git lfs install` before the first LFS file is pulled, or they receive a
# 130-byte text pointer named foo.png and Unity imports it as a broken
# texture.
#
# *.wav  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.png  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
# *.ttf  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- documents
#
# Found while committing the Activity 10 report, and worth writing down
# because it had already damaged a file. Git's built in attributes give a PDF
# `diff=astextplain` but never mark it binary, so with `core.autocrlf=true`,
# which is the default on Windows, Git treats a PDF as text and rewrites every
# LF byte inside it as CRLF on checkout.
#
# The proof is in this repository: the working copy of
# Docs/Daquis_Group33_FirstRelease.pdf had a different checksum from the blob
# it was checked out from. The blob is fine; the file on the Windows disk was
# not. These lines stop it happening to the next one.
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.pptx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
